'\"
.TH "rbc::GRAPHINST" 3tcl 0\&.5\&.0 "rbc" "Upgraded Tcl/Tk9\&.0-ready RBC package"
.SH NAME
rbc::GRAPHINST - Commands in namespace rbc::GRAPHINST
.SH "GRAPH WIDGET INSTANCE GRAPHINST COMMAND"
.PP
Commands in this namespace document operations accepted by graph, barchart, and stripchart widget instances\&.
.PP
GRAPHINST is used below as a placeholder for the actual widget command:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
graph \&.g
\&.g configure -title "Example"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same widget instance interface is shared by all three graph types\&. The main difference is the default
element type used by the element operation:
.TS
tab(|) box;
l l.
Widget created by | Default element type
_
graph| line
barchart| bar
stripchart| strip
.TE
.PP
Explicit line and bar operations are also available independently of the widget's default element type\&.
.PP
Graph widgets contain several components with their own command interfaces:
.TS
tab(|) box;
l l.
Operation| Component
_
axis| virtual axes
xaxis, x2axis, yaxis, y2axis | axes mapped to graph margins
element| elements of the widget's default type
line| line elements
bar| bar elements
marker| graph markers
pen| drawing pens
legend| graph legend
grid| graph grid
crosshairs| interactive crosshairs
postscript| PostScript output
.TE
.PP
These component interfaces are documented separately\&.
.SH "COORDINATE SYSTEMS"
.PP
Graph widgets use two coordinate systems:
.TP
\(bu
\fBgraph coordinates\fP are the numeric data values represented by the axes
.TP
\(bu
\fBwindow coordinates\fP are pixel positions within the widget\&.
.PP
The transform operation converts graph coordinates to window coordinates, while invtransform performs the
reverse conversion\&.
.PP
The general transform and invtransform operations use the graph's primary X and Y axes\&. To transform a
value using a particular axis, use that axis's transform or invtransform operation\&.
.SH "WIDGET OPTIONS"
.PP
The following options configure the graph widget itself\&. They are shared by graph, barchart, and
stripchart and may be specified when the widget is created or modified later with GRAPHINST configure\&.
.PP
Some options, such as -barmode, -barwidth, and -baseline, primarily affect bar elements but are available
on all three graph widget types\&.
.TS
tab(|) box;
l l.
Option| Description
_
-aspect ratio| Sets a fixed width-to-height aspect ratio for the plotting area\&. A positive value causes one dimension of the plotting area to be reduced as necessary to maintain the requested ratio\&. A value less than or equal to zero disables aspect-ratio enforcement\&. The default is 0\&.0\&.
-background color| Sets the background used for the outer graph area and margins\&. The plotting area has its own -plotbackground option\&. -bg is a synonym\&.
-barmode mode| Controls how bar elements having the same X coordinate are arranged\&. Valid modes are normal, infront, aligned, overlap, and stacked\&. normal is equivalent to infront\&. The default is normal\&.
-barwidth width| Sets the default width of bar elements in graph-coordinate units\&. The value must be finite\&. Values less than or equal to zero are normalized to 0\&.1\&. The default is 0\&.8\&.
-baseline value| Sets the baseline from which bars are drawn\&. The value must be finite\&. The default is 0\&.0\&.
-borderwidth width| Sets the width of the 3-D border around the outside of the widget\&. The appearance of the border is controlled by -relief\&. -bd is a synonym\&.
-bottommargin size| Sets the requested size of the bottom margin\&. A value of 0 lets the graph calculate the required size automatically\&. -bm is a synonym\&.
-bottomvariable varName| Names a global Tcl variable that is updated with the actual size of the bottom margin after graph layout\&. An empty value disables the variable update\&.
-bufferelements boolean| Enables caching of the plotted elements in a backing pixmap\&. This can improve redraw performance when elements remain unchanged while markers, legends, or other overlays are updated\&. The default is 1\&.
-buffergraph boolean| Enables double-buffering of the complete graph\&. When enabled, the graph is first rendered into a pixmap and then copied to the widget window, reducing visible redraw flicker\&. The default is 1\&.
-cursor cursor| Specifies the mouse cursor displayed over the graph widget\&. The default is crosshair\&.
-data string| Stores application-defined data associated with the graph widget\&. The graph rendering code does not interpret this value; it may be used by Tcl code or bindings to associate additional information with the widget\&.
-datacommand command| Stores an application-defined command string associated with the graph\&. The graph core currently does not invoke this command automatically\&.
-font fontName| Specifies the font used for the graph title\&.
-foreground color| Specifies the foreground color of the graph title\&. -fg is a synonym\&.
-halo distance| Sets the default maximum screen distance used when searching for a closest data point or element\&. Operations that provide their own halo value override this setting\&. The default is 2m\&.
-height distance| Specifies the requested height of the graph widget\&. The default is 4i\&.
-highlightbackground color | Specifies the color used for the traversal highlight area when the widget does not have keyboard focus\&.
-highlightcolor color| Specifies the color used for the traversal highlight when the widget has keyboard focus\&.
-highlightthickness width| Specifies the width of the keyboard-focus highlight around the widget\&. The default is 2\&.
-invertxy boolean| Swaps the placement and orientation of the logical X and Y axes\&. When enabled, X axes are displayed vertically and Y axes horizontally\&. The default is 0\&.
-justify justify| Specifies the justification of multi-line graph title text\&. Valid values are left, center, and right\&. The default is center\&.
-leftmargin size| Sets the requested size of the left margin\&. A value of 0 lets the graph calculate the required size automatically\&. -lm is a synonym\&.
-leftvariable varName| Names a global Tcl variable that is updated with the actual size of the left margin after graph layout\&. An empty value disables the variable update\&.
-plotbackground color| Specifies the background color of the plotting area\&. This is independent of the outer -background color\&. The default is white\&.
-plotborderwidth width| Sets the width of the 3-D border surrounding the plotting area\&. Its appearance is controlled by -plotrelief\&.
-plotpadx pad| Sets horizontal padding around the plotting area\&. pad may contain one screen distance, applied to both sides, or two distances specifying the left and right padding separately\&. The default is 8\&.
-plotpady pad| Sets vertical padding around the plotting area\&. pad may contain one screen distance, applied to both sides, or two distances specifying the top and bottom padding separately\&. The default is 8\&.
-plotrelief relief| Specifies the 3-D relief of the plotting area\&. Valid values are the standard Tk relief values\&. The default is sunken\&.
-relief relief| Specifies the 3-D relief of the outer graph widget\&. Valid values are the standard Tk relief values\&. The default is flat\&.
-rightmargin size| Sets the requested size of the right margin\&. A value of 0 lets the graph calculate the required size automatically\&. -rm is a synonym\&.
-rightvariable varName| Names a global Tcl variable that is updated with the actual size of the right margin after graph layout\&. An empty value disables the variable update\&.
-shadow shadow| Specifies the shadow used when drawing the graph title\&. An empty value disables the title shadow\&.
-takefocus focus| Controls whether the graph participates in keyboard focus traversal\&. It has the same interpretation as the standard Tk -takefocus option\&. The default is an empty value\&.
-tile image| Specifies an image used to tile the background of the graph margins\&. An empty value disables tiling and uses the normal -background instead\&.
-title text| Specifies the graph title\&. An empty value suppresses the title\&.
-topmargin size| Sets the requested size of the top margin\&. A value of 0 lets the graph calculate the required size automatically\&. -tm is a synonym\&.
-topvariable varName| Names a global Tcl variable that is updated with the actual size of the top margin after graph layout\&. An empty value disables the variable update\&.
-width distance| Specifies the requested width of the graph widget\&. The default is 5i\&.
.TE
.SS "BAR MODES"
.PP
The -barmode option determines how bars from different elements are arranged when they use the same X
coordinate\&.
.TS
tab(|) box;
l l.
Mode| Description
_
normal| Equivalent to infront\&. This is the default\&.
infront | Draws each successive bar in front of the previous bar\&.
stacked | Stacks successive bars vertically\&.
aligned | Places bars side by side\&.
overlap | Places bars side by side with a small overlap\&.
.TE
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
barchart \&.b
\&.b configure -barmode stacked
.fi
.RE
.PP
The -barmode, -barwidth, and -baseline options have no significant effect unless bar elements are present
in the graph\&.
.SS MARGINS
.PP
The graph has four margins surrounding the plotting area\&. Their sizes are normally calculated automatically
from axes, axis titles, tick labels, the graph title, and the legend\&.
.PP
The margin options:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
-bottommargin
-leftmargin
-rightmargin
-topmargin
.fi
.RE
.PP
override the automatically calculated size when set to a positive screen distance\&. Setting a margin size to 0
restores automatic sizing\&.
.PP
The corresponding options:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
-bottomvariable
-leftvariable
-rightvariable
-topvariable
.fi
.RE
.PP
can be used to observe the actual calculated margin sizes\&. After layout and drawing, Rbc writes the current
size of each configured margin to the specified global Tcl variable\&.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
graph \&.g -leftvariable leftSize -bottomvariable bottomSize

pack \&.g -fill both -expand yes
update idletasks

puts "left margin: $leftSize"
puts "bottom margin: $bottomSize"
.fi
.RE
.SS "PLOTTING AREA"
.PP
The outer graph background and the plotting-area background are configured independently\&.
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
graph \&.g  -background grey90  -plotbackground white  -plotrelief sunken  -plotborderwidth 2
.fi
.RE
.PP
-plotpadx and -plotpady add space immediately around the plotting surface\&. Each option accepts either one
distance:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g configure -plotpadx 10
.fi
.RE
.PP
or two distances for the two sides:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g configure -plotpadx {5 15}
\&.g configure -plotpady {10 20}
.fi
.RE
.SS "ASPECT RATIO"
.PP
A positive -aspect value constrains the plotting area to a requested width-to-height ratio\&.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g configure -aspect 1\&.0
.fi
.RE
.PP
requests a square plotting area, while:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g configure -aspect 1\&.5
.fi
.RE
.PP
requests a plotting area whose width is 1\&.5 times its height\&.
.PP
Rbc satisfies the requested ratio by reducing the usable width or height of the plotting area; it does not
enlarge the widget\&. Set -aspect to 0 to disable the constraint\&.
.SS BUFFERING
.PP
Two separate buffering mechanisms are available\&.
.PP
-buffergraph controls double-buffering of the complete widget\&. With double-buffering enabled, Rbc renders the
graph to an off-screen pixmap and copies the completed image to the window\&.
.PP
-bufferelements controls an additional backing store containing the plot region and data elements\&. It is
useful when elements themselves are unchanged but the graph must frequently redraw objects placed above them,
such as markers\&.
.PP
Element buffering is only used when graph double-buffering is also enabled\&.
.PP
For graphs whose element data or axes change very frequently, disabling -bufferelements may avoid maintaining
a cache that cannot be reused\&.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g configure -buffergraph yes -bufferelements no
.fi
.RE
.SS "APPLICATION DATA"
.PP
The -data option is an application-defined storage slot\&. Rbc stores the supplied value but does not interpret
it in the C graph implementation\&.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g configure -data [dict create source simulation1 quantity voltage]

set graphData [\&.g cget -data]
.fi
.RE
.PP
-datacommand similarly stores a command string\&. It is retained as part of the graph configuration interface,
but the current graph core does not automatically execute it\&.

